const aurelinTales=[
 {title:'The Elves and the Shoemaker',pages:[
 '“Draw close,” says Aurelin, folding his silver-tipped wings. “Before this kingdom had towers, a poor shoemaker possessed enough leather for one last pair of shoes. He cut it carefully, left it on his bench, and went to bed wondering how he and his wife would live. At dawn, the shoes were finished. Every stitch was so fine that a customer paid more than the asking price. The shoemaker could buy leather for two pairs.”',
 '“Each night he prepared the leather. Each morning beautiful shoes waited for him. Two pairs became four, and four became eight. He prospered, but he did not forget the mystery. One night, he and his wife hid beside the workbench. Two little elves slipped into the room. They climbed onto the bench and stitched, tapped and hammered until the work was complete. Their clothes were poor, yet their care was magnificent.”',
 '“The couple wanted to thank them. His wife made tiny shirts, coats and trousers; the shoemaker made little shoes. They left the gifts on the bench instead of leather. When the elves returned, they dressed themselves with delight, danced through the workshop and departed. They never returned to sew there again. But the shoemaker continued to prosper. Remember this: gratitude did not mean demanding one more night of unpaid work. Love let the helpers leave.”'
 ]},
 {title:'After the Shoemaker’s Happily Ever After',pages:[
 '“Now comes an original continuation,” Aurelin tells the children. “Fine coats did not answer every question. The two elves walked away from the shoemaker’s house and discovered that freedom could be frightening. They had spent so long making things for others that they scarcely knew what they wanted for themselves. At the forest’s edge, one wished to build a home. The other wished to travel.”',
 '“They disagreed beneath a willow. Neither was wicked; each was afraid of losing the other. At last they made a promise: one would build a workshop beside the river, and one would return each spring with stories, seeds and unfamiliar patterns. Their first customers were travelers whose boots had worn through. When a traveler could not pay, the elves asked for a song, an hour of help, or nothing at all.”',
 '“Years became generations. The workshop’s prosperity became the Silverstitch inheritance. But Aurelin warns that wealth alone cannot preserve kindness. The descendants must choose, again and again, whether their doors open only for the powerful or for anyone whose feet are tired. Eldoria honors the first elves by keeping their craft alive. Its happiest ending is a beginning renewed each morning.”'
 ]},
 {title:'The Eight Silver Stitches',pages:[
 '“This is Eldoria’s own family chronicle,” Aurelin says quietly. “At a crowded kingdom festival, eight children from the Silverstitch line became lost among music, lanterns and carriages. People took advantage of their confusion. They were carried to another kingdom and placed in Briar House, where adults changed names, concealed records and traded children into wealthy households.”',
 '“Their parents never stopped searching. The children grew apart, remembering fragments: a silver button, a familiar tune, a shoe stitched with an unusual pattern. Those memories were not proof by themselves, but neither were they foolish. A memory could lead to a witness. A witness could lead to a ledger. A ledger could expose a lie. No single hero held every answer; the truth survived because many people kept a piece of it.”',
 '“The reunion did not make the missing years disappear. The grown siblings had their own loyalties, grief and hopes. Their parents had to learn who they had become. That is why our lanterns honor both the children who were lost and the adults who returned. Read the full Silverstitch story in your storybook, and follow its adventure when you are ready. A family found again still needs time to become a family together.”'
 ]},
 {title:'Cinderella After the Glass Slipper',pages:[
 '“Here is another imagined tomorrow,” Aurelin begins. “Cinderella married the prince, but palace life brought questions the slipper could not answer. Courtiers praised her beauty while speaking sharply to the servants. She recognized the sound of that cruelty. It was the same sound she had heard in her old kitchen, dressed now in finer words.”',
 '“She asked the prince to walk through the palace before sunrise. Together they watched cooks light fires, laundresses haul water and stablehands tend the horses. He had known their jobs; he had not understood their days. Cinderella did not demand that everyone become a princess. She demanded that nobody’s dignity depend on becoming one.”',
 '“Change came slowly. Some nobles complained when servants were allowed rest, schooling and a fair hearing. Some promises proved easier to make than to keep. Cinderella learned to listen before deciding, and the prince learned that affection was no substitute for doing the work. Their happily ever after endured because they practiced kindness in rooms where no ballroom audience could applaud.”'
 ]},
 {title:'The Sleeping Kingdom Wakes',pages:[
 '“Imagine a kingdom waking after a hundred years,” Aurelin says. “The princess opened her eyes, but so did the cooks, gardeners, children and guards. Beyond the thorn hedge, roads had changed. Old friends were gone. People spoke of the sleeping castle as a legend. A kiss had broken the spell; it could not explain the new world.”',
 '“The princess called for maps, but the oldest gardener asked for seeds. The cook asked where flour could be found. A child asked whether there would still be a school. These were the questions that awakened the kingdom a second time. Neighbors beyond the hedge brought food and taught the sleepers what had happened while they dreamed.”',
 '“Some days the princess mourned the life she had missed. Some days she planted roses. She learned that bravery could mean accepting help, and that rebuilding a home was an adventure of its own. No one had to pretend the long sleep was harmless. Happiness grew because the kingdom made room for grief as well as celebration.”'
 ]},
 {title:'The Owl and the Promise of Tomorrow',pages:[
 '“Why do your citizens cherish this tree?” asks a small elf. Aurelin turns one golden eye toward the river. “Because this was once the place where travelers waited to learn whether the broken crossing was safe. I could see the water from these branches. The first elves listened when I warned them. Later, they returned to tell me what lay beyond it.”',
 '“The tree became a meeting place. Farmers brought accounts of the harvest. Children brought impossible questions. Queens came without crowns when they needed honest company. Nobody owns the memories gathered here. A story is cherished when it is shared carefully, and a sacred place is cared for by the people who use it.”',
 '“Leave the roots undisturbed. Let the fireflies keep their little lights. Tell the truth about yesterday, and make room for tomorrow. That is our promise beneath the Memorybough. Your adventure does not end because a tale closes. Step away from my tree, greet a neighbor, mend something, make something, and live the next page.”'
 ]}
];
// Character narration: one cancellable sentence queue, with Aurelin's owl greeting.
const taleNarration={generation:0,timer:null,utterance:null,nodes:[],context:null};
function stopTaleNarration(){taleNarration.generation++;clearTimeout(taleNarration.timer);window.speechSynthesis?.cancel();taleNarration.utterance=null;for(const node of taleNarration.nodes){try{node.stop();}catch{}}taleNarration.nodes=[];}
function taleVoices(){return (window.speechSynthesis?.getVoices()||[]).filter(v=>/^en(?:-|_|$)/i.test(v.lang));}
function taleVoice(role){let voices=taleVoices(),chosen=voices.find(v=>v.voiceURI===state[role+'Voice']);if(chosen)return chosen;let preferred=role==='owl'?/Daniel|George|Arthur|Brian|David|Guy|Oliver/i:/Samantha|Jenny|Aria|Victoria|Karen|Zira/i;return voices.find(v=>preferred.test(v.name)&&/^en-GB/i.test(v.lang))||voices.find(v=>preferred.test(v.name))||voices.find(v=>/^en-GB/i.test(v.lang))||voices.find(v=>v.default)||voices[0];}
function owlGreeting(){const Audio=window.AudioContext||window.webkitAudioContext;if(!Audio)return 0;try{const ctx=taleNarration.context??=new Audio();ctx.resume().catch(()=>{});let start=ctx.currentTime;for(let i=0;i<2;i++){let oscillator=ctx.createOscillator(),gain=ctx.createGain(),at=start+i*.42;oscillator.type='sine';oscillator.frequency.setValueAtTime(i?340:390,at);oscillator.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(i?230:270,at+.29);gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0,at);gain.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(.11,at+.06);gain.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(.001,at+.34);oscillator.connect(gain);gain.connect(ctx.destination);oscillator.onended=()=>{oscillator.disconnect();gain.disconnect();};oscillator.start(at);oscillator.stop(at+.36);taleNarration.nodes.push(oscillator);}return 850;}catch{return 0;}}
function narrateTale(text,role='narrator'){stopTaleNarration();if(!window.speechSynthesis||!window.SpeechSynthesisUtterance){toast('Read-aloud is unavailable in this browser.');return;}let token=taleNarration.generation;const chunks=text.match(/[^.!?]+[.!?]+[”’"']*|[^.!?]+$/g)||[text];let i=0;function next(){if(token!==taleNarration.generation||!$('modal').open)return;if(i>=chunks.length){taleNarration.utterance=null;return;}let sentence=chunks[i++].trim(),u=new window.SpeechSynthesisUtterance(sentence),voice=taleVoice(role);if(voice){u.voice=voice;u.lang=voice.lang;}else u.lang='en-GB';u.rate=role==='owl'?(sentence.includes('?')?.93:.87):.96;u.pitch=role==='owl'?(sentence.includes('?')?1.12:sentence.includes('!')?1.03:.92+(i%3)*.035):1;u.volume=1;taleNarration.utterance=u;u.onend=()=>{if(token===taleNarration.generation)taleNarration.timer=setTimeout(next,role==='owl'?290:160);};u.onerror=()=>{if(token===taleNarration.generation)stopTaleNarration();};window.speechSynthesis.speak(u);}let delay=role==='owl'?owlGreeting():0;taleNarration.timer=setTimeout(next,delay);}
function taleAudioControls(text,role){if(!window.speechSynthesis||!window.SpeechSynthesisUtterance)return;action(role==='owl'?'Listen to Aurelin':'Listen to the storyteller',()=>narrateTale(text,role));action('Stop narration',stopTaleNarration);const select=document.createElement('select');select.setAttribute('aria-label',role==='owl'?'Aurelin’s voice':'Storyteller voice');select.style.maxWidth='100%';const hint=document.createElement('p');hint.textContent=role==='owl'?'Aurelin’s voice · wise, whimsical, and owly':'Storyteller voice';function populate(){if(!select.isConnected)return;select.innerHTML='';let automatic=document.createElement('option');automatic.value='';automatic.textContent='Automatic storyteller voice';select.append(automatic);for(const v of taleVoices()){let option=document.createElement('option');option.value=v.voiceURI;option.textContent=v.name+' ('+v.lang+')';select.append(option);}select.value=state[role+'Voice']||'';}select.onchange=()=>{stopTaleNarration();state[role+'Voice']=select.value;persist();};$('modal').append(hint,select);populate();window.speechSynthesis.addEventListener?.('voiceschanged',populate,{once:true});}
const narrationModalBefore=modal;modal=function(...args){stopTaleNarration();narrationModalBefore(...args);};$('modal').addEventListener('close',stopTaleNarration);$('modal').addEventListener('cancel',stopTaleNarration);

