const owlGuideBefore=renderUI;renderUI=function(){owlGuideBefore();$('quest').insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend','<div class="card"><small>THE SACRED MEMORYBOUGH</small><p>Meet Aurelin, the cherished owl in gold spectacles. Visit his treehouse for fairy tales and the lives that follow happily ever after.</p><button id="visitOwl">Visit Aurelin →</button></div>');$('visitOwl').onclick=visitMemorybough;};

// Original continuations, separate from the user's preserved manuscripts.
aurelinTales.push(
{title:'Rapunzel and the Open Door',pages:[
'“When Rapunzel finally had a home with doors,” Aurelin says, “she discovered that an open door could still frighten her. In the tower she had dreamed of roads and crowds. Now she sometimes longed for quiet. Her husband mistook her silence for unhappiness with him, until she explained that freedom needed practice.”',
'They made a little garden where visitors knocked before entering. Rapunzel began teaching songs to children, then chose a market day to venture beyond the gate. Nobody insisted she climb every mountain simply because she could. She learned a road at a time, with room to turn back.',
'Years later she funded a house for people with nowhere safe to go. Its doors opened from both sides. “You may stay,” she told its first guest, “and you may leave.” Her happily ever after was measured in choices, not in the length of her hair.'
]},
{title:'Snow White’s Council of Seven',pages:[
'“Snow White’s wedding did not end her friendship with the seven dwarfs,” says Aurelin. “When palace advisors proposed opening a mine beneath an old forest, she invited her friends to explain the cost. They knew stone, water and the darkness under roots. The advisors knew only the price of jewels.”',
'The council argued for days. Snow White refused to make her friends decorations at court or to assume that royal people knew best. A survey revealed that the mine would poison a stream feeding three villages. The kingdom changed its plans and paid the workers to repair an abandoned quarry instead.',
'The seven returned home with fair wages and the right to be heard. Snow White visited without a procession whenever she could. In that small cottage she remembered that rescue had begun with hospitality. She made that lesson part of the kingdom’s laws.'
]},
{title:'The Frog Prince and the Pond',pages:[
'Aurelin tilts his spectacles. “After the enchantment broke, the prince avoided the pond. He disliked remembering his small green body and the years when nobody listened to him. Then a dry summer came, and the palace gardeners proposed draining the pond to fill a royal fountain.”',
'The prince visited at dusk. Frogs called from the reeds. He remembered finding shelter there, and suddenly the plan seemed unbearable. He asked the gardeners to save the pond and let the fountain fall silent. Water was carried first to gardens that grew food.',
'When the rain returned, he placed benches beside the reeds. There was no statue of himself. “I learned kindness while I was small,” he told the princess. “I should not forget it because I am powerful again.” The frogs continued their noisy council beneath the moon.'
]},
{title:'Beauty and the Quiet Garden',pages:[
'“When the Beast became a prince, Beauty expected the castle to feel familiar,” Aurelin begins. “But old servants returned, visitors arrived, and the prince found himself praised by people who once feared him. He was tempted to pretend the lonely years had never happened.”',
'Beauty kept a corner of the rose garden quiet. There they spoke honestly about what had hurt them and what they had learned. She loved him, but she would not let kindness excuse anger. He began apologizing without asking for praise, and listening without waiting to defend himself.',
'The castle opened a library for neighboring villages. Guests no longer needed fine clothes to borrow a book. Beauty planted roses along the public path. Their happiness grew through small habits: a truthful conversation, a respected boundary, and a welcome offered freely.'
]},
{title:'Hansel and Gretel’s Safe Hearth',pages:[
'“Hansel and Gretel grew up knowing how a beautiful house could hide danger,” says Aurelin. “Gretel became a baker. People asked why her shop displayed its ingredients plainly instead of building a wonderful candy doorway. She answered that hungry children needed food before they needed a spectacle.”',
'Hansel repaired roads through the woods. At confusing turns he put markers that could not be eaten by birds. Together they established a supper table where nobody had to surrender a name or promise work before receiving a bowl of soup.',
'Some nights the old fear returned. The siblings did not call that failure. They lit the hearth, checked the door, and sat together until morning felt possible again. Their safe home was built from trustworthy people as much as timber.'
]},
{title:'Goldilocks Makes Amends',pages:[
'Aurelin smiles. “In this imagined continuation, Goldilocks came back to the bears’ cottage with a carpenter and a basket of oats. The smallest bear hid behind his father. Goldilocks realized that her adventure had been somebody else’s frightening afternoon.”',
'She apologized without claiming she had meant well. The family decided what help they would accept. She paid for the chair, replaced the food, and stayed outside until invited. Forgiveness was theirs to offer; it was not another thing she could take.',
'Later she helped build a travelers’ shelter with signs explaining where guests could rest. The bears sometimes joined its supper table. Friendship came slowly. When Goldilocks finally entered their cottage again, she knocked, waited, and was welcomed.'
]},
{title:'Rumpelstiltskin’s Unspoken Name',pages:[
'“After the queen saved her child,” Aurelin says, “the kingdom celebrated her cleverness. But she remembered the impossible demand that had started everything: turn straw into gold or lose her life. A victory over one bargain did not make that demand just.”',
'She ordered that no servant or tradesperson be punished for refusing impossible work. The king resisted until she asked whether he would accept such a command himself. The court began hearing complaints it had once silenced. Some were uncomfortable; all deserved an answer.',
'The queen taught her child that names were not weapons and promises made under fear required careful judgment. In the nursery, straw became toy animals instead of gold. It was finally allowed to be enough as it was.'
]},
{title:'Red Riding Hood Knows the Road',pages:[
'“Red Riding Hood did not stop loving her grandmother,” Aurelin begins, “but she learned that kindness and caution could walk together. Their village repaired the forest road and agreed on places where travelers could ask for help. Nobody blamed a child for a deceiver’s choice.”',
'As she grew older, Red carried food to neighbors herself. She listened carefully when strangers claimed to know her family. She checked an unexpected message before following it. The woods remained beautiful; they were not required to become frightening forever.',
'Her grandmother planted flowers beside the cottage gate. On visiting days they shared stories with children beneath the trees. Red’s favorite lesson was simple: you can be generous without giving everyone your trust.'
]},
{title:'Hamelin Hears Its Children',pages:[
'Aurelin’s voice becomes gentle. “This is a new imagined continuation of Hamelin, not a promise about how every older telling ends. After years of searching, the town found a path beyond the mountain where some of its missing children had grown into adults. They remembered the unpaid Piper—and the parents they had lost.”',
'Reunion brought questions the mayor’s speeches could not settle. Some wished to return. Some had built lives elsewhere. The town offered homes, records and restitution without making forgiveness a condition. It preserved accounts from those who had suffered, even when the accounts embarrassed its leaders.',
'The empty schoolhouse became a place where children could speak at public meetings. Hamelin learned that keeping a promise was only the beginning. A town had to listen before the people with the least power disappeared from its attention.'
]},
{title:'The Little Mermaid’s Own Horizon',pages:[
'“Let us imagine a different tomorrow,” Aurelin says, “in which the young mermaid survives her costly bargain and returns to the sea with her voice. At first she is ashamed of everything she risked. Her sisters remind her that wanting another life was never shameful. Losing herself to obtain it was the wound.”',
'She travels along the coast and becomes a keeper of songs. She learns sailors’ melodies without pretending to belong to every ship, and teaches sea songs without demanding that anyone abandon land. Love becomes a meeting between lives, rather than the erasure of one.',
'At sunset she sings where sea and shore can both hear her. There is no prince waiting to complete the song. Her own voice carries it. Aurelin closes his eyes. “Sometimes happily ever after begins when you are allowed to choose a horizon for yourself.”'
]}
);

